review_comments.body:
@AndrewJSchofield : Good point. Claude suggested the following way to tighten this up.

```
RequestContext is the only production caller of toResponseHeader() (:159, :173). Compute both the body version and the header version from the same supported version:

public Send buildResponseSend(AbstractResponse body) {
    short version = apiVersion();
    return body.toSend(responseHeader(version), version);
}
public ByteBuffer buildResponseEnvelopePayload(AbstractResponse body) {
    short version = apiVersion();
    return body.serializeWithHeader(responseHeader(version), version);
}
/**
 * The response header follows the version the response is written at, which for an unsupported
 * ApiVersionsRequest is v0 rather than the version of the request.
 */
private ResponseHeader responseHeader(short responseVersion) {
    return new ResponseHeader(header.correlationId(), header.apiKey().responseHeaderVersion(responseVersion));
}
```

As a related issue, currently, KafkaApis uses 4 different ways to obtain the api version. They all agree except for unsupport ApiVersion.
```
Accessor | Returns | Why
-- | -- | --
request.header.apiVersion | N (6) | data.requestApiVersion(), the version in the request header
request.context.apiVersion() | 0 | isUnsupportedApiVersionsRequest() is true, so it returns 0, the version the response is written at (RequestContext.java:180-185)
request.context.requestVersion() | N (6) | header.apiVersion(), returned as an int (RequestContext.java:217-219)
request.body[ApiVersionsRequest].version | 0 | parseRequest builds new ApiVersionsRequest(new ApiVersionsRequestData(), (short) 0, header.apiVersion()) (RequestContext.java:131), so the body is a v0 request and its body isn't parsed
```

 It would be useful to document what each api means and when to use which.

We probably can change request.context.apiVersion() to sth like request.context.effectiveApiVersion() and document it that this is the version parsed by the server. KafkaApis should typically use this or request.body[ApiVersionsRequest].version to get api version.

Document that request.context.requestVersion() is the way for getting the original Api Version sent by the client. This should only be used in the following in KafkaApis where the original version is needed.

`if (!apiVersionManager.isApiEnabled(request.header.apiKey, request.header.apiVersion))
`

